From 6dd18ae4aee5fb103a99293088a9e9ccbc908726 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Mutai <official.daniel.mutai@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 09:56:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(inventory): auto-fill product details on unmatched
 barcode scan

When a scanned barcode has no match in the org's own inventory, the
Add Product sheet now makes a best-effort lookup against Open Food
Facts (free, no API key) to prefill name/brand/category. Expiry date
is intentionally NOT part of this - standard EAN-13/UPC-A barcodes
(what's on typical duka/kiosk goods) don't encode expiry or batch
data, only a product ID; that would require GS1-128/DataMatrix
encoding, which is out of scope here. See hisaflow-ocr-scanning-plan.md
Section 0 for the original analysis this builds on.

Backend:
- InventoryService.lookupExternalProduct(): calls Open Food Facts,
  4s timeout via AbortController, never throws - any miss (not
  found, malformed response, network error, timeout) resolves to
  null so the frontend can treat 'no match' as a normal outcome.
- GET /inventory/barcode/:code/external - authenticated (any org
  member), no org-scoping needed since it queries a public dataset,
  not org data.

Frontend:
- lookupExternalProductByBarcode() in inventory.service.ts, mirrors
  the apiGet pattern used elsewhere.
- AddItemSheet: on open with a scanned barcode, fires the lookup and
  prefills name/category/variant name only if those fields are still
  empty (never overwrites something the user already typed). Shows a
  status banner: loading / 'found via Open Food Facts, please verify'
  / 'no match, enter manually'. Lookup runs alongside the existing
  barcode-prefill effect and is fully non-blocking - a failed or slow
  lookup never prevents manual entry.

Coverage caveat (documented in code + will need to be understood by
the user): Open Food Facts skews toward US/EU packaged food products,
so many local Kenyan/duka goods will simply miss and fall back to
today's manual-entry behavior. This is a convenience prefill, not a
data source to trust blindly - UI copy says 'please verify'.

Verified with tsc --noEmit on the frontend workspace (0 errors).
Backend change could not be tsc-verified in the dev sandbox (Prisma
engine binaries blocked by sandbox network policy, pre-existing
limitation unrelated to this change) - reviewed manually; mirrors the
existing ocr.service.ts fetch() pattern exactly, no Prisma or schema
changes.
